From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: gaokun3: lower the DMIC clock to 3.2 MHz

At 4.8 MHz, a value shared with the ThinkPad X13s, the DMICs capture dense
impulses with peaks of 0.5 to 0.8 of full scale at the +16 dB that
audio-route.sh sets. DMIC0, DMIC1 and DMIC2 show them all the time. DMIC3
shows far fewer, in the first second of a capture and while sound is
playing. They come in before the digital gain and do not depend on the
decimator mode. Applications record DMIC0 and DMIC1, so they get this noise.

The steady impulses stop at 3.2, 2.4 and 1.6 MHz. Take 3.2 MHz, the highest
of them. The noise above 16 kHz rises as the clock falls, about 2 dB overall
from 3.2 to 2.4 MHz and 7 dB to 1.6 MHz, and stays the same below 8 kHz.
Pitch does not change and the level stays within about 1 dB up to 4 kHz.

The microphone part is unknown, so this does not tell whether the limit
comes from the microphones or from the board.
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8280xp-huawei-gaokun3.dts | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8280xp-huawei-gaokun3.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8280xp-huawei-gaokun3.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8280xp-huawei-gaokun3.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8280xp-huawei-gaokun3.dts
@@ -1489,7 +1489,7 @@
 
 	vdd-micb-supply = <&vreg_s10b>;
 
-	qcom,dmic-sample-rate = <4800000>;
+	qcom,dmic-sample-rate = <3200000>;
 
 	status = "okay";
 };
